// Probe: A PIPED CALL CONTRIBUTES NO ROWS TO AN ASSERTION'S REPORT.
//
// This is a real divergence between the two halves of the interpreter, found by
// having to lower both: `evalCall` records every argument into the assertion
// trace (eval/eval.go:4899 and :4922) and `evalPipe` never does. So
// `assert double(1 + 2) == 7` shows `1 + 2 = 3` and
// `assert (1 + 2) |> double() == 7` shows nothing, for the same call with the
// same argument.
//
// The arguments are written as expressions rather than literals on purpose: a
// literal operand is suppressed by the shared "an operand that reads exactly
// like its value explains nothing" rule, so a literal-argument fixture would
// print the same thing on both paths and pin nothing at all. That is the trap
// the first draft of this file fell into.
//
// Both spellings PASS and FAIL identically, so only a FAILING assertion can see
// the difference — which is why every assertion here fails on purpose. The
// cases sit side by side so the reports can be read against each other: the
// only difference between them is the pipe, and the only difference between the
// reports is the `values:` block.
//
// What this fixture pins is that the compiled path REPRODUCES the asymmetry. A
// backend that "improved" it would make every piped assertion failure report
// differently from `nomi test`, which is the one output this project treats as
// byte-for-byte contract.
fn double(n: Int): Int {
    n * 2
}

fn scaled(n: Int, factor: Int): Int {
    n * factor
}

test "a directly written call shows its arguments" {
    assert double(1 + 2) == 7
}

test "the same call through a pipe shows none" {
    assert (1 + 2) |> double() == 7
}

test "a piped stage's own extra arguments are not shown either" {
    assert 3 |> scaled(2 + 3) == 7
}

test "a call nested inside a piped stage's argument still shows its own" {
    assert 3 |> scaled(double(2 + 3)) == 7
}
